{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n21 \n \n \nhardware for the first E-FIELD Flames tests because of the initial use of the same coflow burner.  \nPlanning is critical because crew activity is scheduled three weeks in advance, for which detailed \nprocedures must be prepared and uplinked to the ISS. \n \nAs a correction, including two maintenance activities, there were 11 crew days in Sept.-Oct. 2017 \nto prepare for the initial CLD Flame testing on the space station. \n \nAll CIR tests are remotely commanded from the ground from NASA\u2019s Glenn ISS Payload Operations \nCenter (GIPOC) in Cleveland, Ohio.  Typically, four to six people staff four consoles during the \ntesting.  The CIR hardware is controlled from the Rack Officer (RO) and Data Management Officer \n(DMO) consoles, and the ACME hardware is controlled from the Payload Developer (PD) console.  \nTwo shifts of personnel are scheduled to staff these three consoles on test days because they \ntypically last 14 hours with the many pre- and post-testing activities.  Given that the tests are \nremotely commanded, the operations are carried out in the day and evening in Cleveland, e.g., \nfrom 7 AM to 9 PM.  A day\u2019s tests, i.e., with flames, are normally conducted in a period of 10 hours \nof less.  Tests are normally conducted on three weekdays per two weeks.  On intervening \nweekdays, the image data is downlinked and the crew often swap one or both gas bottles.  \nTransferring and downlinking data from a day\u2019s tests also requires a 2-shift day for the RO and \nDMO consoles, where it is always necessary to verify that data was downlinked without errors \nand that data is not missing.  Those two positions are also staffed to guide and oversee the crew \nactivity.  As a result, the CIR consoles are normally actively staffed on four weekdays per week.  A \nteam of four carry out the detailed planning for the CIR and ACME console staff, and the daily \noperational plans typically exceed 50 pages on days including testing. \n \nThe science console allows for review of the analog video and non-imaging telemetry (at 1 Hz) in \nnear real time but doesn\u2019t allow for commanding.  In the case of E-FIELD Flames, arrangements \nwere made so that the analog video is streamed to a secure computer at UC Irvine to allow the \nresearch team to participate in test operations and to recommend adjustments to the day\u2019s \nplanned parameters.  That team does not see the telemetry during the testing, but is in", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 21}}